**ai-teammate** (2026-10-06):

🚨 **BLOCKING: the one-liner bundle silently hangs on iOS/Android for CodeMie / ChatGPT / AIIN**

`sso_desktop_flows_io.dart` is selected by `export ... if (dart.library.io)` — which is true on **every mobile platform too**, not just desktop. On iOS/Android the core flows' `openBrowser` falls through to `return false` (it only shells out to `open`/`xdg-open`/`start`), so the flow:

1. logs "could not open browser automatically; open this URL manually: …" — but fa_ui's `onStatus` is `_logStatus`, a `kDebugMode`-only `debugPrint`, so in a release build **nothing is printed anywhere**;
2. waits out the full 5-minute loopback-callback timeout;
3. returns null — the picker just sits there, no snack, no page, no entry.

Meanwhile `_hasSsoFlow` enables the tiles whenever `sso != null`, on every platform. A mobile thin-client host that follows the advertised AC2 one-liner (`sso: FaUiSso(registry: registry)`) ships three sign-in tiles that are visibly enabled and silently dead — a regression from the pre-PR behavior (tiles hidden) and against the issue's own option-C principle ("an absent flow is visible and explained, never silent"). Copilot is unaffected (device flow needs no browser hop); CodeMie/ChatGPT/AIIN are.

The PR scope note discloses desktop-only delivery, but the mobile failure mode is worse than "prints the URL": it prints to a debug-only channel. Please gate the hops so the existing, already-tested snack path handles it, e.g. in this file:

```dart
const _desktop = bool.fromEnvironment('dart.library.io') && _isDesktopOs; // or:
Never _requireDesktop() {
  if (!Platform.isMacOS && !Platform.isLinux && !Platform.isWindows) {
    throw UnsupportedError(
      'sign-in needs a desktop platform (a browser + localhost callback '
      'server); pass your own WebView-backed callback on this platform.',
    );
  }
  return;
}
```

With that, `_runDesktopFlow`'s `on UnsupportedError` snack ("…not available on this platform.") fires on mobile, and a mobile host that wired the bundle still gets an explained, disabled-looking outcome instead of a silent hang. A widget test asserting the snack on a forced `TargetPlatform.android` (mirroring the existing `UnsupportedError` test) would lock it in.

